From: lmoresi <louis.moresi@anu.edu.au>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 14:35:24 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] add_fault keeps a vertex on each tip, and the chain must
 end on them

add_fault pulled the trace's samples in order, and when two choose one
vertex the later keeps it. A first tip within reach of the next sample
went without, the cut's first segment ended on that sample, and the fault
was added one sample spacing short of its tip: all 36 faults of the
480-trace box grid that stopped short of a tip stopped short at the START
(0.31 and 0.625 cells at sampling 0.3, 0.5 at 0.5), and their slip was a
median 10 % off for shortfalls under 0.4 cells. test_0897's "empty" and
"short" traces were this too.

The tips are now pulled after the other samples, so each keeps its
vertex, and that vertex is labelled (the pull writes the tip's own
coordinates; the cut carries the label). add_fault then refuses a chain
whose ends are not those vertices, joined globally by vertex number: an
exact check, by mesh point, with no tolerance.

On the grid: refusals by distance 69 -> 20 of the one-piece faults, the
median slip error against the trace sampled a cell apart 5.0 % -> 2.0 %,
and no added fault stops short of a tip (the 12 still short are 150-degree
hairpins that lose a whole arm, refused by the distance and the ends).

test_0897: the two traces are now split whole, a trace sampled half a cell
apart keeps both tips (fails before: 0.5 cells short), and a hand-labelled
chain ending off a tip is refused. Suites: 432 passed, 1 skipped.

